                                                    <div id="Button4" class="tabcontent">
                                                        <h3 class="factitle" style="text-align: center; font-size: 22px;">Teaching Innovation</h3>
                                                        <p class="facpara" style="text-align: center;">
                                                            <strong>From Hangar to Classroom<br>
                                                                Teaching International Business by Living It</strong>
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="facpara" style="text-align: center;">
                                                            <strong>Faculty: International Business — PGDM
                                                                Programme</strong>
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            Thirty-five years of working across continents in the global
                                                            aviation industry — managing supply chains that span North
                                                            America, Europe, Russia, Israel; negotiating procurement
                                                            contracts in dollars, euros, roubles, and rupees; signing
                                                            off on quality dossiers under FAA, EASA, DGAQA, and DGCA
                                                            scrutiny; and steering operations through 9/11, the 2008
                                                            downturn, the volcanic ash crisis, and a global pandemic —
                                                            has given me a single, deeply held conviction: international
                                                            business cannot be taught from a textbook alone. It must be
                                                            taught the way it is lived. My pedagogy for PGDM students is
                                                            built on this conviction.
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            I teach through what I call the EVOLVE approach —
                                                            Experience-led, Visual, Outcome-oriented, Live,
                                                            Voice-of-industry, and Ethics-anchored. Every concept I
                                                            introduce in class first travels through a real story from
                                                            my career: an AOG (Aircraft on Ground) crisis where a
                                                            24-hour cross-border procurement decision saved an airline
                                                            USD 2 million; a Letter of Credit dispute with a European
                                                            OEM that taught me more about Incoterms than any chapter on
                                                            the subject; or a freight-forwarder consolidation that
                                                            turned a loss-making spares operation into a margin-positive
                                                            one. These are not anecdotes. They are case studies the
                                                            students dissect, debate, and rebuild — with me as both the
                                                            protagonist and the discussant.
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            International procurement is taught not as a theory of
                                                            make-or-buy, but as a sequence of judgement calls — supplier
                                                            qualification, total-cost-of-ownership modelling, currency
                                                            hedging, dual sourcing, geopolitical risk scanning, and
                                                            contract negotiation across cultures. Students work on live
                                                            RFQs adapted from my industry network, evaluate bids,
                                                            prepare commercial offers, and defend their recommendations
                                                            before mock procurement boards. Strategy is taught through
                                                            the lens of decisions I have made and watched others make —
                                                            entry-mode choices for MRO partnerships Europe, USA,
                                                            joint-venture structuring in Russia and Israel, and
                                                            competitive responses to low-cost carrier disruption. The
                                                            frameworks of Porter, Kraljic, Ghemawat's CAGE, and
                                                            Ghoshal–Bartlett come alive when they are tested against
                                                            real boardroom dilemmas.
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            Supply chain is taught as a living system. Students map
                                                            end-to-end flows of an aircraft component from a foundry in
                                                            Italy to a maintenance hangar in Bengaluru — covering lead
                                                            times, customs codes, AOG urgency, warehouse positioning,
                                                            and reverse logistics for repairable items. Documentation,
                                                            often the dullest topic in textbooks, is reframed as the
                                                            connective tissue of global trade. We spend full sessions on
                                                            commercial invoices, packing lists, certificates of origin,
                                                            airway bills, dangerous goods declarations, and Form
                                                            1/8130-3 airworthiness tags — because a missing signature on
                                                            the wrong document grounds an aircraft and costs a fortune.
                                                            Logistics is taught with route maps, mode-mix decisions,
                                                            customs broker simulations, and the trade-offs between sea,
                                                            air, courier, and bonded movement.
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            I keep classes interactive — fish-bowl debates, role-plays
                                                            where students play buyer, supplier, freight forwarder, and
                                                            customs officer in the same negotiation, guest sessions with
                                                            serving CXOs from my network, and field visits to airports,
                                                            bonded warehouses, and freight terminals around Greater
                                                            Noida and the Jewar corridor. Assessment blends traditional
                                                            exams with industry projects, supplier-evaluation reports,
                                                            country-entry plans, and capstone international expansion
                                                            strategies.
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            Above all, I teach with humility. Aviation taught me that a
                                                            single overlooked detail can ground a fleet; international
                                                            business is no different. My role is not to deliver answers,
                                                            but to equip future managers with the discipline, the
                                                            judgement, and the global instincts to find their own —
                                                            wherever in the world their careers may take them.
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="facpara">
                                                            <strong>From Hangar to Classroom — Teaching the World, One
                                                                Decision at a Time.</strong>
                                                        </p>
                                                    </div>


